# M5 union roster correction — frozen author handoff

Candidate `aef9e78c34dca6897ddfcbfb439246edf76dd364`, exact parent donor `da39c439898aec1233aa8b6157b35e961df1e453`; branch `codex/v03-m5-union-roster-correction`, isolated tree `/private/tmp/kodezart-v03-m5-union-roster-correction`. Four files: one production coordinator and three tests, 628 additions/28 deletions. Writer frozen and worktree clean. Requested high effort is inherited; no claim about effective model effort is made. Independent root acceptance remains required.

## Requirement and preserved provenance

Fresh reads of KOD110, KOD77, KOD78 and their complete comment pages are captured in `m5-union-live-requirements.json`. The governing bounded defect is [F02](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-110#comment-9db9ee7f-5b95-4725-bef5-6021b59d848e), independently revalidated at [donor360](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-110#comment-437ec69a-6ea8-4523-a750-6774561eae39): union participation is the retained scope lane roster, not the dispatch-ready set. Share planner ordering, not dispatch eligibility. The current KOD110 outcome/fix requires complete open lane heads, planner order, scratch-only composition, native-head freshness and a scope-level observation distinct from lane outcomes. [S1-M5](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-110#comment-10352dc8-b743-441d-87c5-69a107162015) and [W-B15](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-110#comment-312385cf-c448-49d9-94a8-9b74a937036e) preserve check-chain, no-publication and union-red routing requirements. These are recorded tracker rulings/reviews attributed to their actual authors, not newly verified direct-human quotations.

The exact current PR114 remains OPEN at `c17f5d71ce95a94d0fd63f74ee6aa467cce01dad`; its body/comments were freshly read into `m5-union-pr114-current.json`. Its [blocking review](https://github.com/YalDan/kodezart/pull/114#issuecomment-5644876872) remains preserved: useful real-Git order/conflict/cleanup tests coexist with a wrong no-ready oracle and unapproved scanner/holdings claims. No PR metadata or discussion was modified. Historical union branches were inspected; none had an active union worktree. The old lane-delivery worktree was e7ae430 and was left untouched.

Current [KOD588](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-588) retains the earlier mutation counterexample: a conflict-only cleanup leak was not caught by the cited ordering suite. Current [KOD587](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-587) still needs the real scope walk-tick invocation; [KOD593](https://linear.app/duckburg/issue/KOD-593) still needs durable union residual and terminal consumption. This patch does not relabel any of these Done.

## Source correction and return boundary

`chains/delivery_coordinator.py` now exposes `ScopeUnionCoordinator`, naming its actual responsibility separately from the unchanged `LaneDeliveryCoordinator`. There is no compatibility alias or competing owner. At the exact parent, no application constructor selected the old class: its call sites were tests. Renaming and exercising this shared constructor is not proof of M3 application wiring.

The coordinator reads existing `read_scope_plan`, takes every ordinary native scope lane, and excludes only existing structural classifications (`criterion`, `RECORD_KINDS`). Approval, open criterion gaps and blocking dependencies do not discard retained participants. Existing `plan_topology` receives complete scope/dependency facts, the complete candidate set and explicit empty blocking predicate so its effective-priority/age policy orders all participants. No second sort, approval resolver, classifier or future walk schedule was introduced. The same planner still detects dependency cycles; missing dependency facts and cyclic parentage refuse before Git work.

The existing `_lane_branch` method is unchanged AST: every ordinary participant must carry exactly one deliverable ref or gets the existing typed `UnionHeadReadError`. Missing refs are not silently omitted, assumed landed or replaced by trunk. KOD777's unrelated ref-less landing fork remains untouched. The truly empty ordinary participant set gets the same error type with the precise no-participating-lane reason. Current refs are fed into unchanged `UnionTick`, which obtains current native head SHAs and uses unchanged pinned composition/freshness/cleanup logic.

After measurement, the coordinator rereads the same complete ordered branch roster. A changed scope membership or branch reference raises typed `UnionHeadReadError` before returning the observation to a terminal/grader caller. This is observed readback, not an atomic tracker/Git transaction or an assertion that facts cannot change after the final read. It adds no actual scope-terminal consumer, lane outcome arithmetic, status write or merge authority.

Only constructor naming, imports/docstrings, complete roster selection and this return guard change in production. Constructor body and required-ref lookup remain exact parent AST. M3 native execution, shared protocols/configuration/composition roots, M4 event/state, actual lane delivery, topology/planning, UnionTick, UnionComposition and union/terminal result types are byte-identical to parent.

## Regression and PR114 harvest

The donor `test_a_scope_the_planner_ranks_nothing_in_refuses_to_compose` is replaced by a requirement-grounded real-Git oracle. Both criterion families are closed while refs remain; each branch's check is run independently and passes; their actual combined tree fails. The result must contain both lane heads in planner order, one root remediation and a removed scratch tree. Two mixed completed/unfinished controls require both branches exactly once. All three failed against unchanged da39; log `m5-union-retained-before.log`. With membership fixed, eight coordinator cases passed in 35.42s (`m5-union-retained-after.log`).

Two additional actual runner probes mutate native membership or a recorded branch while the composed check runs. Both failed before the post-observation guard (`m5-union-roster-readback-before.log`: 2 failed, 8 deselected, 12.38s), and pass in the final boundary selection. Further cases retain unapproved heads, reject an actually empty structural-only roster, refuse parent/dependency cycles and refuse missing dependency facts rather than dropping nodes. Missing/duplicate deliverable-ref tests remain.

PR114's seven concrete exit scenarios are extracted into `tests/chains/test_union_exit_invariance.py`: green, merge conflict, pathless conflict, unclassifiable check chain, undeclared chain, unobservable runner, and repeated cancellation during owned worktree creation. Existing donor/PR helpers set up actual author/remote/observer repositories and publication-fatal Git methods. Each scenario directly checks all three repositories' refs unchanged, created worktrees removed, paths absent and the native worktree registry empty apart from the observer. The conflict path now has explicit removal evidence rather than relying on another path's success. PR114 AST exit inventory, line tracing and recursive holdings-walk additions are not imported. The base's existing isolation test file is unchanged; no new scanner is presented as behavioral proof.

PR114's inherited-priority/age order distinction is retained and strengthened in `test_union_participant_order.py`. The original four-lane rival-order oracle is extended with the urgent blocked ordinary member carrying its own fifth retained branch. The complete order is `c,e,a,d,b`, while dispatch readiness still excludes e (`c,a,d,b`). Native merge-parent traversal, rather than only a port call log, proves the actual scratch order. Alphabetical/opened, reverse and own-priority ordering differ. Separate `tracker`/closed `decision` artifact cases prove non-lane classification exclusion without inventing a readiness filter.

